\f0\fs24 \cf2 The fireplace in the Bevilacqua Room is one of those pieces you simply can\'92t miss. Elegant, monumental, and above all, steeped in history. Its design is inspired by a Tuscan Renaissance model, documented in a historic photograph preserved in the museum\'92s archives.\
Every detail, from the scrolling side elements to the angels holding crowns, down to the floral vases, has been reproduced with almost obsessive care. But this isn\'92t a mere replica: it\'92s a loving reinterpretation, just as the Bagatti Valsecchi brothers always did. During their travels, they photographed, took notes, and let themselves be inspired. And when something truly captivated them, it became part of their home.\
That\'92s the case with this fireplace, a faithful echo of that Renaissance model they must have fallen for at first sight. But one detail tells us something more: in the center, where the original featured a rampant lion, here you\'92ll find an eagle. It\'92s the heraldic symbol of the Bagatti Valsecchi family. A silent, yet unmistakable signature. Because even when they were copying, they always made it their own.}